[{:seon.task/name "program-cost-follows-what-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "A merge from Datahike's index diff, O(changes x log V), exists only outside this repository: commit `0823517` \"seon.merge/three-way-db\" in the tag `history/l3/next/main` (its library-level numbers in the outer repository's the archived paper option3-L3-libs.md (git history), Item 1), which needs the datahike fork's branch ``jolt/merge-diff`` (`021e876`, `8311254`: `datahike.db/datoms-diff` on Jolt), not yet in this repository's datahike module. Fetch both before those directories are archived. What exists: `seon.merge/program` reads every live form and definition of a db (O(program), not O(change)), and the three-way merge's callers build it on base, ours and theirs per merge, per :branch-diff and in `seon.reconcile/judge-tx`. Reproduction: with Seon's own source on main (54 files, 1,168 forms, 2,278 program keys) `seon.merge/program` took 256 ms a call in the test runtime (443 ms on 1,641 forms in a release root, `seon.program/live-forms` 368 ms of it), so every run pays about 1 s of whole-program reads whatever it changed; `seon.e2e/loop-turn!` took 13.4 s from release to quiet (implement 4.8 s, merge 6.9 s), against a target under 1 s. Correct: a branch's changes since its merge base come from Datahike's history between base and head (the datoms its transactions touched), main's the same way, and only those keys are read on base, ours and theirs. Replaces the whole-program reads in three-way's callers (`seon.writer` branch-merge, `seon.reconcile/judge-tx`, the dashboard's diff); `program` stays for tests and mg/check. Done by worktree session global-ids (2026-10-23): seon.merge/delta-three-way reads the keys the datoms that differ bear (datoms-diff over shared trees, touched-keys, program-of); 6.7 s -> about 7 ms on a 115k-datom main (docs/storage.md), flat from 100 to 10,000 forms; every seon.merge-test case checks it answers what three-way does. Callers moved: the writer's branch-merge and :branch-diff, seon.agent/merge-main!, seon.turn's contract gaps, seon.merge/unmerged (now [db]); merges then carry the branch's own datoms (seon.merge/merge-tx)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-cost-test/a-three-way-costs-the-changes-not-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "conflict-value-states-its-input"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): `seon.merge/conflict-value` decodes a pulled merge entry; its contract takes `:seon.merge/entry-members` (the :seon.merge/* members, generating as a pulled map) instead of `:any`, under which mg/check generated nil or 0 and the answer could not be a conflict map. Its callers pass a Datahike entity (`seon.merge` entries) or a pulled map (`seon.problems`, the run's conflicts; `seon.task`). Proof: mg/check green on this function, the callers' tests green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-conflict-value-reads-a-pulled-entry}}
 {:seon.task/name "main-wins-a-conflict"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when an agent merges main (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and both sides changed a key, `seon.merge/three-way` names the conflict, the branch keeps its own content, and the conflict stays an unmerged entry (`seon.merge/unmerged-tx`) until the branch redefines the key or resolves it (`seon.merge/resolve-tx`); docs/parity.md: ours is kept. v1 (MERGE-STEP 5a): main's content wins on the branch and the branch's own is kept on the entry (:seon.merge/kept), so the agent runs main's definition and re-applies its change knowingly. Correct: merge-main! applies theirs for a conflict too, the entry holds :seon.merge/kept, and `seon.merge/unmerged` keeps it open until the branch resolves it. The attribute's name waits on the owner's D6 ruling, shared with seon.merge/a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-conflict-on-merging-main-runs-mains-definition-and-keeps-ours}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a merge task merges main into the work branch whole (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and the writer merges the whole branch, scratch helpers included. Owner, 2026-10-22: nothing moves between branches by default. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3, prototyped: triple conflict, triples copy, nope missing, no scratch carried): :seon.task/carry ([:var sym] [:schema k] [:datum e a]), :seon.task/source, :seon.task/target; the merge branch starts at the target's head; each item judged against the merge base as missing, same, copy or conflict; a carried function's branch-only callees not carried are missing (v1: a reach-chosen list missed 4 chain-only helpers); a conflict entry records the side the branch holds (:seon.merge/kept) so `seon.merge/unmerged` stays open until resolved. Deletes: the merge task's whole-branch merge-main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-merge-task-judges-each-item seon.merge-test/an-uncarried-branch-only-callee-is-missing}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store" :seon.task/plan "Whether a Claude worktree branch can merge through the store (the design in docs/merging.md; the review docs/merging.md says: on hold until experiment 3's diff is empty). Measured (the scratch paper merge-probe-2026-10-27.md (git history); probe root on 4840ddb's first parent, jolt/data-attrs-from-the-index imported by `seon.source/refresh-tx`): experiment 1 holds on correctness: :ours of `seon.merge/delta-three-way` is exactly the definitions git's diff touches (10 vars, 16 forms), 0 conflicts, 67 datoms written for the branch, `seon.source/files` byte-equal to git show tip for both files; but it cost 12,607 calls and 277 MB, not about 39 and under 600 KB (main's side changed 79 keys since the base, and the values were d/db-with copies, not store branches: the number on real d/branch! branches is not measured). Experiment 2 fails: the merged program's 917 tests all plan to run, 0 reused from test-results/ that bin/prove --merge proved green; the first differing digest is datahike.query-test/a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused (import 11baa001 against 5 stored greens). Cause, partial: every digest takes :env, which digests `seon.fx/config-input`, the whole config in force less :seon.config/place keys; the probe root's config was not bin/test's (a --set of agent max-running, its own --config layer); restoring that key changed the digest and still left 917. Next probe: compare bin/test --plan's per-test digests on 4840ddb's tree with the import's, one by one. Experiment 3, not run: on a worktree root (its own store, never runs/store, :seon.config.source/branch set to a probe-only branch so write-back cannot touch jolt/merges or jolt/seon-merges), attach a task, hand back a past branch that touched src, test, default.edn and a bin script and deleted something, through seon_attach and seon_done; measure whether the judge ran on the imported code (the review: the agent's hand-back runs the proofs on its loaded program, so the import must be the agent's own, as `seon.agent/load-tests!` does, before hand-back), what the verdict tree held against git show tip (default.edn, bin and deps.edn are not in the store), and the write-back commit's tree diff against the branch tip, file modes included (`seon.fx/git-commit` writes every file 100644 and cannot delete). Routing Claude worktrees through the store waits until that diff is empty." :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/an-outside-branch-writes-back-its-own-tree}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-merge-system"
  :seon.task/plan "The open design (plan direction 3; this task holds docs/merging.md and its review docs/merging.md): one merge system from what Seon already has, not two. Facts (main 4840ddb153): since 2026-10-20 main took 301 first-parent merges, all by git (worktree merges and bin/prove --merge); Seon's own merge (octopus plus write-back) made 14 Seon merge commits, all only on jolt/seon-merges (the review: 0 in main). Duplications the audit named: two mains (git and the store, synced by seon.source/refresh-tx on watch and by write-back plus bin/update); two verdicts (the judge defers process tests and runs them only through my.root/gate!; bin/prove --merge runs every key with no green) and a third runner, seon.prove, the test service (the review: the design never says what becomes of it, and the :tests effect would need a new contract and a lock); two serializers (bin/env's flock and the reconciler's one merge flight); two records (facts and the merge log, now the merge commit message); a merge task per work task (done: seon.reconcile/a-work-task-merges-itself-through-the-one-gate); three branch-vs-base diffs (seon.merge/delta-changes, seon.program/divergence, seon.program/diff-plan, the last for loading). Owner rulings that bound it: Seon's merge path stays whole and is exercised before anything is cut (do not throw out the baby); a work task merges itself; worktrees retire. Review corrections to apply to the design before anything is built: octopus and merge-keys are not dead under max-running 1 (that caps only :merge-kind starts; whole tasks in one judge pass merge as one octopus), so cutting to one branch is a behaviour change; gate-checkout is set by no local.edn (the gate has never run on the standing root); the branch-key mismatch is worktree roots writing the shared ref (done: bin/env names jolt/seon-merges-<worktree>); the import must be the agent's own before hand-back (seon.agent/load-tests!), not root's after it; the verdict tree must be the branch's own tree because default.edn, system.edn, tasks, bin and deps.edn are not in the store; seon.fx/git-commit writes mode 100644 and cannot delete (a bin script loses its executable bit, deletions are routine), so pin bumps, native code and deletions stay git merges until it can; a Seon merge commit with no branch parent makes the branch look unmerged and drops its measured commit message; a synchronous seon_done running process tests on every merge overturns the 2026-10-23 process-tests ruling and needs the owner. Order: the deletions that stand alone first (done: file tasks merge themselves; the gate and :unrun problem and the merge-log as a render of facts remain); routing worktrees through the store waits on seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store (experiment 3). Correct: the design names which runner (seon.prove or bin/prove) is the one verdict and deletes the other's verdict role, and carries the two experiments' numbers. Proof to write: after the build, no second verdict path is left (a resolve of the gate's vars is nil) and one result store answers both bin/test and the judge (0 keys to run for a tree bin/prove --merge proved)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store"}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-merges-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"it should be the same sync system\"; \"this is a problematic part we are kinda hardcoding so we need to be clear about what we are doing and maybe even turn this into a config edn param\". Found on md-scan 29: main moved its wiki under a living branch, merge-main carried the code and not the sections, so the branch read batch 42 code against the morning wiki, and seon.problems/caused-split held its done on a catch main named and the branch did not.\n\nWhat exists, four ways to say what a merge carries: (1) the three-way over program keys (seon.merge/program, key-entity: [:form id], [:var sym] and kin, [:schema k], [:datum e a]); (2) for [:datum], a data-attrs argument each caller picks: root api :all (seon.root/request), my.branch/merge-main! and the writer #{} (seon.writer octopus-merge and :merge-candidate), so one merge carries different data by who asks; (3) seon.merge/platform-record?, every seon.* attribute never carried except the whitelist seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs (task plan, instructions, proofs, cause), which the three-way does not read: (4) seon.task/carry-tx carries those four by a second path. Wiki sections are seon.* platform records, so never merged either way.\n\nWhat v1 did (~/src/seon, seon.merge, resources/seon/tasks/seon.program.edn AW0 and seon.merge.edn AW1): each entity schema declared its partition, :seon.program/partition (program, data, cluster), and its conflict rule, :seon.cluster/conflict (:theirs, :ours, :add, unmerged); seon.program/cluster-attributes derived the attributes from the schemas; one three-way with two projections; a carried entity schema with no identity attribute was refused by the schema check. The field: Dolt and git merge rows or lines by their key; Datomic has no merge.\n\nThe design: what merges is declared on the entity schema, as :seon.db/taken and :seon.db/content-id already are (resources/seon/schemas). An entity schema property :seon.merge/carried, one of :file (content of a checkout file: merged by its identity key and written back to its file by the writer: forms, vars, namespaces, methods, impls, schemas, wiki sections), :db (shared facts merged by identity both ways, never a file: a task content an agent edits) or :none (a record of what happened, written where it happened: runs, evals, turns, processes, errors, embeddings, judges, ends, model calls); an attribute may override its entity (:seon.task/execution :none on the task); :seon.merge/conflict, default :unmerged (an entry on the branch, as today), or :theirs, :ours, :add. One reader, seon.merge/carried, derived once from the schemas as seon.agent/taken-attributes is. Loud: an entity schema with no :seon.merge/carried is red in the schema check (never a silent default), and a carried one needs a :db.unique/identity attribute, its merge key [attr value].\n\nWhy on the schema and not a key in default.edn (the owner asked): it is a fact of the attribute, not a limit or default; it sits beside the attribute, so whoever adds an entity declares its merge in the same edit and the schema check refuses one that does not; default.edn would be a second list to keep in step with the schemas. The owner decides.\n\nDeletes: carried-platform-attrs, platform-record?, data-attr? and data-attr-set, the data-attrs argument through seon.root, seon.writer, seon.agent/merge-main! and my.branch (its two defaults), the [:datum e a] key (an identity key replaces it), seon.task/carry-tx (task content merges by the three-way), and seon.merge/wiki-tx (jolt/branch-gets-mains-wiki, not merged).\n\nSteps, each its own branch: (1) the declarations on every entity schema and the reader, no behaviour change: proof that the reader reproduces today (:file is the program attributes, :db is carried-platform-attrs) and that an undeclared entity schema is refused; (2) the three-way reads the reader and the data-attrs argument goes; sections are :file, so main changes reach a branch, and a branch change to a section is refused by the writer with its reason until (4); (3) carry-tx folded into the three-way; (4) the writer writes a changed page back to docs/, once a page round-trips exactly (file to sections to file, seon.docs/page-text). Proofs: seon.merge-test/what-merges-is-declared-on-the-schema, seon.merge-test/a-branch-takes-mains-wiki (from jolt/branch-gets-mains-wiki, kept), seon.schema-test/an-entity-without-a-merge-declaration-is-refused, the merge-main and task-content merge tests unchanged."
  :seon.task/kind :work}]
